Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement:
“Material Adverse Effect” means any change, event, condition, occurrence or effect that (i) has had, or would reasonably be expected to have, a material adverse effect on the business, assets, liabilities, results of operations, or financial condition of ABC Corporation and its Subsidiaries; provided, however, that for purposes of subclause (i) the following effects are excluded from such determination: 
   (a) fluctuations in general economic conditions or general market volatility;
   (b) broad-based changes in regulatory, legislative or judicial interpretations of applicable Law, including changes in generally accepted accounting principles;
   (c) variations in capital availability, including changes in interest rates, credit ratings or currency exchange rates;
   (d) any effects attributable solely to customary seasonal fluctuations or non-recurring natural disasters; and 
(ii) any event, change, or effect that would, or would reasonably be expected to, prevent, materially delay or materially impair the ability of ABC Corporation to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement; provided that no carveouts or exceptions set forth in subclause (i) shall apply in respect of subclause (ii).

Please answer based solely on the above text.
Choices:

