Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement:
"Material Adverse Effect" means any change, event, or occurrence that has, or would reasonably be expected to have, a material adverse impact on the business, financial condition, or operational results of the Company and its Subsidiaries. In determining whether a Material Adverse Effect has occurred, particular attention must be given to the Company’s ability to consummate the Transactions contemplated hereby. Notwithstanding the foregoing, any delay or impairment in the Company’s ability to consummate the Transactions that arises solely from:
   (i) general changes in global economic conditions or fluctuations in interest and currency exchange rates;
   (ii) standard market volatility or general declines in securities trading volumes;
   (iii) Force Majeure events, including natural disasters, pandemics, or other broadly affecting occurrences;
   (iv) delays solely attributable to the public announcement or routine regulatory review of this Agreement;
shall not be deemed to constitute a Material Adverse Effect. However, any additional or disproportionate impact on the consummation, beyond the direct effect of these carveout events – especially if the impairment is unique to the Company relative to its industry peers – may be considered under a Material Adverse Effect analysis. The parties expressly agree that, for purposes of this Agreement, the impairment in the ability to consummate that is attributable only to the carveout factors enumerated above shall not trigger an MAE.
(Pages 22-25)
